From: Mohtashim <mohtashim9119@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 01:34:17 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(ws2-44): a deduplicated zip import no longer leaks its
 asset blob

extractZipToAssets persists every file it reads to IndexedDB before any
dedup decision is made, so the merge loop's name-collision branch dropped
the Asset from project.assets but left the row it had already written.
Every deduplicated import therefore leaked a blob that nothing would ever
reference or reclaim.

The loop is extracted to services/zipAssetMerge.ts and now deletes the row
it drops. Two properties are preserved exactly: dedup runs against earlier
survivors of the same archive as well as existing assets, and voiceoverId
still takes the LAST surviving audio asset. The extraction is what makes
the fix testable at all -- mounting <App /> in jsdom was costed and
rejected previously (docs/history.md).

Deleting is safe because the id is minted inside extractZipToAssets and
never escapes the merge: a dropped asset reaches neither project.assets,
nor an undo-history snapshot, nor voiceoverId. audioAssetId is reported
only for survivors, so a dropped row can never become voiceoverId -- the
sibling processZipFile path does NOT hold that property and is left
untouched this round (see session report).

Gates: tsc/lint clean; vitest 2966 passed / 0 failed / 77 skipped (+6);
gaplessInvariant 36/36; golden replay 6/6; K13 3/3. src-tauri untouched.
Probes: removing the deleteAsset call reds exactly the 3 row-accounting
tests; leaking a dropped audio id into audioAssetId reds exactly the
voiceoverId guard; deleting unconditionally reds 5 of 6.
